/Users/alexis/.claude/skills/project-management/README.md:292:- local mode reads policy from root `spawn`; remote mode reads `remote.spawn`, falling back to root `spawn` when `remote.spawn` is missing or `null`. Both locations use the identical surface: `enabled` defaults to `true`, `max_parallel` defaults to `20`, optional `preferred_agentic_system`, optional UTC `schedule`, legacy `preferred_agent`, default-on `launch_composition.enabled`, and optional versioned per-agent `ceilings` with role overrides. The canonical provider system is a tagged union: `{"exclusive":"codex"}` pins one provider, while `{"mixed":["claude","codex"]}` permits the normalized set. `schedule.windows[]` uses start-inclusive/end-exclusive `start_utc` / `end_utc` values in `HH:MM` and a per-window `preferred_agentic_system`; later-to-earlier bounds cross midnight (for example `17:00` to `03:00`). Windows must be disjoint. An active window replaces the base provider system, while gaps use the base system or unrestricted runtime affinity when no base exists. An explicit `--agent` and any registered requested model must belong to the active set; violations are refused before side effects with the allowed providers and a retry step. The selected provider's ceiling applies afterward. `max_parallel` schedules runners only within one client work directory; it is not a remote-board or cross-client limit. `project_config().spawn.config_key_prefix` names the effective source, `project_config().spawn.preferred_agentic_system` reports the currently effective mode/set/provenance, `project_config().spawn.schedule` reports UTC windows plus the active window, and `project_config().spawn.launch_composition.config_key` names the active composition field. `spawn-policy-v3` makes each normalized `allowed_models` array the sole admission authority, uses only the exact optional `resolution_model` as fallback, makes effort direction explicit, and reports field provenance for provider and merged role policy. `PolicyRank`, registry order, lifecycle, recommendation, and price have no v3 admission effect. For grandfathered v2 configs, `model_criterion: equal` pins an exact registered model, while legacy/no-criterion, `less_or_equal`, `greater_or_equal`, and effort-only membership comes from the immutable versioned rollout snapshot rather than mutable capability ranks. New model IDs are not auto-admitted until explicit v3 migration. Role keys and `--role` values are trimmed and case-folded before lookup; `coordinator` shares the `orchestrator` policy identity, while path-like or unknown roles fail closed before selection. Ordered criteria require a one-line `--selection-rationale`; an adjusted pair additionally requires exact `--ack-resolved <model>/<effort>` after re-evaluation with fresh rationale text.
/Users/alexis/.claude/skills/project-management/README.md:293:- `task-board q 'project_config()'` is the migration audit surface. V3 reports `contract_version=spawn-policy-v3`, `authority=explicit_allow_set`, normalized provider policy, merged role-effective policy, confirmation mode, effort direction, and exact field provenance. V2 reports `authority=v2_compatibility_snapshot`, snapshot version, exact `snapshot_allowed_models`, stable migration warning reasons, and a copy-ready per-provider/per-role v3 `migration` object. Effort-only v2 policy uses reason `spawn_policy_v2_effort_only_migration_required` and explicitly tells operators to omit `resolution_model` unless they intentionally want a fallback. These objects are read-only evidence: setup, install, query, and spawn never edit a repository config. Migrate one repository at a time: capture full and role-targeted projections, copy the projected objects under `contract_version: spawn-policy-v3`, review the diff, query both surfaces again, then smoke one admitted request and one refusal or acknowledged fallback.
/Users/alexis/.claude/skills/project-management/README.md-294-- `features` is a typed, closed registry: unknown feature names or fields fail closed with the exact dotted key. Each configured feature object requires boolean `enabled`; optional `description` falls back to canonical built-in text, while setup preserves and `project_config()` marks differing operator text as an override. Local mode reads root `features`; remote mode reads `remote.features`, falling back to root when absent or `null`. Setup does not materialize a remote copy, so a later root flag flip remains effective on the next remote launch. New risky capabilities enter default-off and must check the gate at their real runtime consumer. Flags are reread on the next spawn/launch with no cache clear or reinstall, and disabling a feature must leave created state readable and unstranded. See [Config Feature Gates](references/config-features.md).
/Users/alexis/.claude/skills/project-management/README.md-295-- `features.agent_context` is presentation-only: it never weakens board, lifecycle, provider, model, or review gates. It defaults to enabled with `profile=full`; `profile` accepts `full|lite`, and nested `prompt`, `queries`, and `responses` remain strict enums. `enabled=false` ignores the configured profile and overrides and applies built-in full defaults, so disabled is not `lite`. The legacy top-level `agent_context` block remains readable unchanged for one compatibility release; setup does not migrate it, and configuring both forms fails closed with a keep-one diagnostic. Full prompts retain the full role template and body except for a legacy Markdown `Definition of Done` section: role baseline DoD is rendered once from frontmatter, merged with the task checklist. `lite` retains concrete FIRST/LAST commands, merged DoD, task-scoped outcome evidence, reviewer verdict branches, Stop-The-Line, task inputs, and workdir while removing generic CLI/reporting narrative. Its default `prompt.preconditions` policy is `{"mode":"budgeted","inline_bytes":2048}`. Set `mode:"inline"` for normal text inlining. `--context-profile full|lite` selects a complete profile while the feature is enabled; when the gate is off, an explicit override is refused before spawn state is created.
/Users/alexis/.claude/skills/project-management/README.md-296-- `features.context_sharing` defaults to false. It is projected as `declared-but-unimplemented` with `runtime_active=false` until STORY-260728-km72uk ships, even if configured true. Turning it off leaves already-registered contexts readable and simply prevents their use.
/Users/alexis/.claude/skills/project-management/README.md-297-- `features.primary_session_goals` and `features.spawned_run_goals` default to **true**. They are two independent kill switches over the one unified board-goal-v2 model, not a re-split of the contract: they gate only where goals are applied. Unlike a declared-but-unimplemented capability these ship working, so the flags exist to switch a misbehaving plane off in the field. Both are fail-closed and every refusal names the flag. With `primary_session_goals=false`, `task-board goal set-primary|update|clear` is refused with `primary_goal_plane_disabled` and `side_effects=false`, `task-board goal get` (including `--history` and single revisions) keeps working and reports the disabled plane, the wrapper still launches while the Session Manager binds no `parent/primary` goal, and a staged `ApplyGoalRevision` reaching the daemon is refused. With `spawned_run_goals=false`, a goal-bound spawn launch is refused before any run or board side effect — whether the intent is an explicit `--goal-scope` or inherited from a goal-bound parent run — `task-board spawn goal upsert` is refused, plain spawns are unaffected, `task-board spawn goal` still reads the active goal, and goal-gated acceptance for an already-bound run stays enforced. Both descriptions state the launch prerequisite in the config itself: goals only reach a Session-Manager-held session started with `task-board codex|claude` (`codex-board` / `claude-board`); a direct agents-infra launch (`codex-infra` / `claude-infra`) receives no goal binding whatever the flag says. Disabling a plane also clears a goal that was already bound to a live provider session, and fails closed if the provider does not acknowledge the clear. Disabling strands nothing — revisions, heads, and lineage stay intact and re-enabling resumes from the current head with no repair. `project_config()` reports both flags with provenance plus a `goals.planes` block and a tri-state `goals.session_manager_held`: `true` when manager ownership is proven, `false` for an ordinary unbound spawn — which projects the plane as `enabled-session-not-manager-held` — and `null` when the markers were stripped and nothing can be concluded, which projects `enabled-session-unconfirmed`. A bare `TASK_BOARD_RUN_ID` is never taken as proof of ownership, because every spawned child carries it.
